// jni-arg-shift.bridge.ctadl -- the JniArgShift boundary, joined by hand.
//
// The A/B counterpart of `Jni:JniArgShift`; see `jni-flow.bridge.ctadl` for what the sibling
// file does and why `--no-jni-bridge` is not optional.
//
// This is the case that makes the port map earn its keep. `nativeCat` is an *instance* native,
// so its implementation takes `JNIEnv *` at 0, the receiver `jobject` at 1, and the declared
// parameters at 2 and 3. The Java side is a DEX: `this` is register slot 0 and the two String
// parameters are slots 1 and 2 (both references, one slot each).
//
// The C body returns `b` and drops `a`, and the Java half calls `nativeCat` twice with the
// taint in the other argument each time -- so exactly one of the two sinks may be reached. An
// off-by-one anywhere below flips both of the case's assertions at once: `expected_lines`
// stops being reached and `unexpected_lines` starts being. A bare call edge with no port map
// would mis-wire the pair silently, which is the whole reason the map is the feature rather
// than a refinement.

bridge(F::arg(0) <-> G::arg(1)),
  bridge(F::arg(1) <-> G::arg(2)),
  bridge(F::arg(2) <-> G::arg(3)),
  bridge(F::return <-> G::return) :-
  fun(F, language in {"dex", "apk"}),
  F = "LJniArgShift;->nativeCat(Ljava/lang/String;Ljava/lang/String;)Ljava/lang/String;",
  fun(G, language = "pcode"),
  !(!fun(G, name = "Java_JniArgShift_nativeCat") && !(fun(G) && G = "Java_JniArgShift_nativeCat"));
